From f66361f1dea1758e948524e315f20d56a6c17f5d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aamir <aamir@tallow-lang.org>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 01:52:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/27] extract: cap the tier's spend, refusing past the cap
 rather than running
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A reachable engine with no ceiling is an unbounded spend, and the tier had none:
`num_predict`/`max_tokens` bounded one request's response, but nothing bounded how
many requests a run could make or how large a prompt could be.

`LlmBudget { max_calls, max_prompt_chars }` is checked BEFORE each call, not
reported after it: past the cap the tier refuses (EngineMisconfigured) instead of
proceeding. A budget that degrades into "kept going" is not a budget.

The numbers come from TALLOW_LLM_MAX_CALLS / TALLOW_LLM_MAX_PROMPT_CHARS, defaulting
to bounded values and recording which source was used, so an undeclared cap does not
claim to be configured and an unparseable value falls back to the default rather
than becoming a silent zero-cap. `calls_made` and `max_calls` are in `tier_state`
and the run-evidence record, so "what did this run spend" is a measurement.

NON-CLAIM, recorded rather than glossed: P1.T6 says to match `budget.rs`. There is
no `budget.rs` in this tree (`rg --files src | rg budget` → nothing), so there was
nothing to match. The cap is the equivalent shape.

Verification status: the cap half is verified offline — the call cap refuses at and
past the limit, an over-cap prompt is refused before sending, defaults are bounded
and labelled, and an unparseable override falls back. The measurement half
(model_calls/page, tokens/page, ms/page on a fixture corpus, and "pages after the
first report 0 model calls") needs a live engine; with no engine configured the
tier is disabled-with-a-reason, which P1.T6 names as a valid completion. The
zero-model-calls-after-page-1 mechanism itself is already covered by
`cached_schema_extracts_at_css_speed_with_zero_model_calls`.
